==============================================================================
E4  does a wider / different representation lower the required order?
==============================================================================
  representation             dim  faithful  rank(g5 - I)  rank(t - I)  mult of 4
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  standard (4)                 4      True             4            1       True
  permutation (5)              5      True             4            1       True
  perm padded to 8             8      True             4            1       True
  perm padded to 32           32      True             4            1       True
  std + std (8)                8      True             8            2       True
  perm + perm (10)            10      True             8            2       True
  std (x) std (16)            16      True            12            6       True
  perm (x) perm (25)          25      True            20            8       True
  std (x) std + std (20)      20      True            16            7       True
  regular (120)              120      True            96           60       True

  every faithful rep has rank(5-cycle - I) = 4 * m, m >= 1 : True   [PASS]
  minimum over faithful reps tested                        : 4

  Character theory says why. For g of order m,
      dim Fix(g) = (1/m) sum_j chi(g^j),
  and for a 5-cycle in S5 this collapses to
      rank(rho(g) - I) = 4 * (# non-linear irrep constituents).
  Faithfulness forces at least one (the only normal subgroups of S5
  are 1, A5, S5, so a rep with no non-linear part kills A5). Hence 4
  is the floor, in ANY dimension -- and the regular rep pays 96.

  => the model's 32-dim head cannot buy a cheaper 5-cycle by picking
     a smarter embedding. Widening the state does not help.

==============================================================================
E5  best approximation strictly below L  (the fixed n_h=3 question)
==============================================================================
  target: 5-cycle, L=4, ||P||_F = 2.2361
      K     residual   relative         source
      0    3.162e+00      1.414      optimized
      1    2.594e+00      1.160      optimized
      2    1.663e+00      0.743      optimized
      3    1.512e+00      0.676      optimized
      4    1.175e-15      0.000   construction

  target: 4-cycle, L=3, ||P||_F = 2.2361
      K     residual   relative         source
      0    2.828e+00      1.265      optimized
      1    2.000e+00      0.894      optimized
      2    1.732e+00      0.775      optimized
      3    9.421e-16      0.000   construction
      4    9.421e-16      0.000   construction

  The best a 3-factor layer can do against a 5-cycle is 68% relative error.
  That is not a near miss. And it compounds: the task is a RUNNING
  product, so a per-token error of that size cannot survive 128
  steps. Whatever fixed n_h=3 is doing to reach 0.54 sequence
  accuracy, it is NOT approximating each 5-cycle with its three
  Householder factors.

  Which leaves three candidates, none of which this file can settle:
    (a) the additive v k^T channel -- DeltaNet's state update is
        S_t = A_t S_{t-1} + v_t k_t^T, and only the A_t half is
        constrained by any of the above;
    (b) 12 heads coding the group jointly, so no single head ever
        represents the permutation;
    (c) it is not tracking at all, just getting most tokens right --
        0.95 per hard token over ~12.8 hard tokens per sequence is
        0.95^12.8 = 0.52, suspiciously close to the observed 0.54.
  (c) is the cheapest to test and should be tested first.

==============================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================
  1. K >= L(g) is forced by rank, not by parity or orthogonality.
     No choice of beta beats it.
  2. Pinning beta = 2 imposes an EXTRA constraint, K = L (mod 2).
     Free beta removes that one. Our gate needs free beta; that
     is now proved, not assumed.
  3. No faithful representation of S5 makes a 5-cycle cheaper
     than 4. Width does not help.
  4. The best order-3 approximation to a 5-cycle is far from
     exact, so fixed n_h=3's partial success needs a different
     explanation -- the additive v k^T channel, or multi-head
     distributed coding. That is the next thing to measure.

  elapsed 31s
